  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 126 PM CDT Sat Sep 26 2026\n\nHeat remains a concern through the first half of the week with above \nnormal temperatures continuing under a persistent ridge of high \npressure. Hopefully, we will see some relief from the heat beginning \nWednesday or Thursday when the ridge is progged to shift east as a \nlong wave trough digs across the western U.S. This will allow \nincreasing moisture to advect across S TX. The moisture combined \nwith embedded short waves ahead of the main long wave trough, will \nbe conducive for convective development. If a cold front can make it \nto S TX Wed/Thu, it will provide strong low level convergence and \nincrease the rain chances even higher with 20-50% chance Wednesday \nand 50-80% Thursday with the highest chances across the Victoria \nCrossroads. Plus, PWATs are forecast to increase to 2.2-2.4 inches. \nThis is almost 2 standard deviations above normal and is conducive \nfor heavy rainfall/localized flooding. However, confidence remains \nlow that a cold front will reach S TX. Latest models show the front \nmoving into Central Texas and remaining north of S TX. Convection \nthat develops over the Hill Country may produce an outflow boundary \n(mimicking a cold front) which could become the focus for heavier \nrain in S TX. If any of these features don't reach S TX, rain \nchances may end up being much lower along with warmer temperatures \nthe latter part of the upcoming week. Given we are still 5+ days \nout, and models have been somewhat consistent with increased rain \nchances, will maintain the higher rain chances and slightly cooler \ntemperatures for now.\n\nAs for minor coastal flooding, the P-ETSS indicates tide levels up \nto 1.8 feet above MSL for Tue-Wed during times of high tide. There \nis low confidence that water will reach the dunes due to light \nonshore winds through Tuesday, 15-25 knots from the south mid-week, \nswell periods of 7 seconds or less, and the moon phase will be \napproaching the 3rd quarter. None of which are favorable for tidal \noverflow. Will continue to monitor trends for any changes.",
